! SEED=92
! GAME=../entharion/zcode-infocom/zork1-german-beta-r3-s880113.z5

# Zork I in German, beta release 3 / serial 880113 is a rare
# translated Infocom build, and the only story in this corpus whose
# vocabulary needs characters the ASCII table does not have.
#
# THIS BUILD WAS NEVER FINISHED
#
# Infocom started a German translation of Zork I in the late 1980s
# and abandoned it before release. What eventually leaked into public
# circulation decades later, and ended up in this corpus, is that
# unfinished beta -- dated January 13, 1988 -- not a shelved-but-
# complete product. That history explains what's found below far
# better than "just a different build" does: the translation itself
# is non-native and carries real spelling and grammar mistakes, the
# Z-machine's parser was built around English syntax and was never
# fully reworked for German noun cases and compound words, and by
# most accounts the deeper reaches of the game were left without
# working code at all -- reputedly close to unwinnable as a result.
# The English bleeding through in a few places here -- an
# unromanized "beautiful jeweled scaraben" describing the scarab, a
# room plainly named "Egyptian Room" while everything around it is in
# German, "The lamp appears a bit dimmer" appearing verbatim -- isn't
# a ZSCII table problem on this interpreter's end; it's the
# translation itself never having been finished.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (Standard 3.8.5)
#
# ZSCII is not ASCII. Codes 155 to 251 are the "extra characters",
# and what each one means is the story's own business: a story may
# carry a Unicode translation table saying so, and one that does not
# falls back to the default table the Standard prints. A German
# story leans on that table for every umlaut and every sharp s.
#
# So "öffne" (open) is not a decorative flourish here. To match
# it against the dictionary the interpreter must turn that o-umlaut
# into its extra-character code, look the word up in that encoding,
# and hand the parser something the story recognizes. Get the table
# wrong, or fall back to ASCII, and the story answers that it does
# not know the word.
#
# THIS IS A FULL PLAYTHROUGH, NOT A TRANSLATED COPY OF ANOTHER SCRIPT
#
# This uses the same SEED=92 as the English zork1-r88-s840726.accept,
# but that does not make the two runs match. Release 3 beta is a
# different compiled story file from release 88 -- different code,
# not just different strings -- so the two builds make their RANDOM
# calls at different points even from an identical command sequence,
# and combat outcomes (the troll, the thief) diverge from the first
# swing on. There is also no ZIL source for this beta in the corpus
# to check mechanics against, unlike zork0's entharion sources. So
# every command below was found live, one at a time, against this
# build specifically -- not copied from the English script and not
# assumed to carry over.
#
# A few vocabulary quirks turned up along the way, worth flagging
# for whoever extends this further:
#
# - Some nouns whose printed text carries an umlaut are not in the
#   dictionary under that spelling: "Skarabäus" needs "skarabaeus",
#   "Schlüssel" needs "schluessel". The accented form fails with
#   "kann nicht von dem Spielvokabular verstanden werden"; the
#   ASCII-digraph fallback is what the parser actually indexed.
#   "Flasche" (bottle) as a direct object fails outright either way
#   -- it only comes along via "nimm alles".
# - DROP is "lass X fallen", not "leg X hin" or "leg X ab" (both
#   unrecognized sentences) and not "wirf X" -- "wirf" is THROW, a
#   different verb that answers "Man kann mehrere Akkusitiv nicht
#   mit 'wirf' gebrauchen" the moment ALL is attempted. "lass alles
#   fallen" is the multi-object drop.
# - UP and DOWN are "rauf" and "runter", not "oben" ("oben" gets
#   "In diesem Satz fehlt das Verb!") and not "unten".
# - Turn on is "mach X an", not "schalte X an" (unrecognized word).
# - AGAIN is "wieder", not "nochmal" or "wiederhole".
# - WAVE is "winke X" ("schwenke" is unrecognized; "schwinge" and
#   "schüttle" both parse but do the wrong thing).
# - PRAY is "bete"; at the Altar it teleports outdoors to the
#   Forest, same as English -- the routes below rely on that.
# - GIVE reads as "gib X zu Y", not "gib Y X" as in English.
# - HEALTH is "diagnose", same as English, and worth checking after
#   any exchange with the thief -- but it costs a turn like anything
#   else, so checking it doesn't pause whatever's already attacking.
# - ATTACK takes a named weapon: "töte X mit Y" -- worth being
#   explicit about, since without one the parser sometimes guesses a
#   weapon the player isn't even carrying and fails outright with
#   "Du hast das Stilett nicht" (that guess was always the thief's
#   own weapon, not the player's, in every case this came up).
#
# One design note that isn't a vocabulary quirk: this build's carry
# limit is tight -- tight enough that the golden coffin can only be
# carried alongside the lamp, nothing else, not even the sword. That
# collides with wanting the sword in hand for the thief. The fix
# below sets the sword and the painting down together in the
# Egyptian Room just long enough to fetch the coffin out and bank it,
# then comes straight back through the Troll Room and the Temple
# complex for them -- the sword is still there when we return, but
# the thief finds the painting first. That's an acceptable trade:
# the painting is an ordinary treasure with no downside to losing it
# to theft, whereas the egg (handled separately, later, with its own
# minimal-exposure trip) is not -- a stolen, unopened egg can't be
# safely opened by the player and its canary is lost for good, while
# a *given* egg gets opened safely by the thief himself, and its
# canary does turn out intact once he's finally beaten (see below).
# So the egg never leaves the player's hands until it's handed over
# on purpose, while the painting is allowed to become collateral --
# and does get reclaimed anyway, once the thief is dead and every
# last thing he ever stole falls out of his sack at once.
#
# THE THIEF FIGHT, AND WHAT ACTUALLY WON IT
#
# A plain sword did not do it. Two separate sword attempts each cost
# the weapon within a swing or two -- once knocked to the floor and
# recovered, once seemingly lifted clean out of the player's hand,
# gone from both the inventory and the room afterward (a straight
# theft, not a drop, confirmed later when it turned up among the
# thief's own hoard). A third attempt, unarmed entirely because nowhere
# safe to fetch another weapon was known yet, was never risked -- the
# game's own line for it is blunt enough to take at face value: "Ein
# Dieb mit den nackten Händen anzugreifen ist Selbstmord" (attacking a
# thief with bare hands is suicide).
#
# The rusty knife lying by the skeleton in the maze was tried once,
# out of curiosity, and it is not a weapon at all -- it is a trap.
# Wielding it against the thief reads the mind of whoever draws it
# and turns their own hand against their own throat: "Als das Messer
# sich seinem Opfer nähert, wird dein Gehirn von einem Willen
# überwältigt, den du nicht meisterst... Das Messer scheint zu
# singen, als es auf brutalste Weise deinen Hals durchschneidet." An
# instant, unsurvivable death, and presumably the actual explanation
# for the skeleton it was found next to. Never pick this one up to
# fight with, in German or in English -- it is cursed by design, not
# a bug in this build.
#
# What finally won the fight was the ordinary "dangerous-looking"
# knife from the Attic table (dropped there early, in the house-prep
# stretch below, then fetched again just before this trip) -- a
# perfectly normal, uncursed weapon, just a plain second option to
# the sword rather than an upgrade. It got knocked from the player's
# hand once too (recovered off the floor immediately, no worse than
# that), and then connected for a clean kill a couple of exchanges
# later. Whether the knife is actually more reliable than the sword
# here, or this was just where the dice landed on this attempt, isn't
# something a single playthrough can tell; having a spare weapon on
# hand for exactly this situation is the real lesson.
#
# The retreat-and-heal loop used between attempts is worth noting
# too, and worth its own caution: "runter" from the Treasure Room to
# the Cyclops Room is always safe regardless of how the visit upstairs
# went, and waiting there heals an injury with no risk of being struck
# again. But it isn't risk-free in every sense -- the thief roams even
# while the player is just standing still healing, and during one
# stretch of waiting he wandered through the Cyclops Room and robbed
# the sword right out of the player's inventory without any distinct
# message calling it out, only the generic "hat dich restlos
# ausgeraubt" a few turns later made it clear it was gone. Anyone
# retreating to heal should check the weapon is still in hand before
# heading back up, not just assume it survived the wait.
#
# Once the thief is actually dead, he drops absolutely everything he
# was ever carrying or had stolen -- from this run, that was the coin
# sack, the sword, the stiletto, the painting, and the egg itself
# (opened, its canary confirmed present and undamaged, since it was
# given rather than stolen), plus the room's own silver chalice,
# which the game explicitly notes is now safe to take. This carry
# limit only allows so much of that at once, so the two spare weapons
# (the stiletto and the attic knife, no longer needed once he's dead)
# get left behind to make room for the painting.
#
# WHERE THIS SCRIPT STOPS, AND WHY THAT'S A DELIBERATE STOPPING POINT
#
# The route down to the thief -- trapdoor, Troll Room, a short known
# stretch of the maze to the skeleton, then on to the Cyclops Room and
# up -- is the only part of this maze mapped out below, and it does
# not reverse. Multiple attempts to find a way back out from the
# Cyclops Room side, with the thief alive and after his death, ran
# into the maze's deliberately identical room text and its explicitly
# one-way passages ("wenn dieser Tunnel das nächste Zimmer erreicht,
# kannst du ihn nicht mehr als Rückweg benutzen") without turning up
# a route back to the Living Room.
#
# The grating *was* found, though, and this is worth recording since
# it settles a question raised while looking for it: from the
# Treasure Room, "runter, nw, süden, westen, rauf, runter, nordosten"
# reaches a "Gitterzimmer" whose description ("Über dir ist ein
# Gitter, das mit einem Schloß, das aus einem Totenkopf und Gebeinen
# gemacht ist, verschlossen ist") is unmistakably the grating room --
# and that route is exactly the English walkthrough's own "d. nw. s.
# w. u. d. ne" from the same starting point, translated word for
# word. So the map itself is identical between this beta and the
# English release, same as the troll-to-skeleton and skeleton-to-
# cyclops routes earlier already suggested. Only the code behind the
# map -- combat, carrying capacity, scoring -- differs. A further
# check even found a one-way link from near the grating back to the
# skeleton (sw, sw, runter lands on the skeleton's lantern and key),
# but no return trip from there back to the grating turned up in the
# time spent looking, so reaching the grating WITH the key in hand
# remains unsolved.
#
# The reason it's hard to just go back for the key is not maze
# navigation, though -- it's scoring. This game computes the player's
# combat strength live from the current score:
#
#     FIGHT-STRENGTH = 2 + SCORE / 70
#
# (STRENGTH-MIN 2, STRENGTH-MAX 7, SCORE-MAX 350, integer division.)
# The leather coin sack sitting next to the key is worth 10 points
# the instant it's picked up; the key itself is worth nothing. Taking
# the key instead of the sack at that carry-weight crunch -- the two
# don't fit together -- costs exactly the points needed to cross a
# /70 bracket, and the thief fight (already close) goes from winnable
# to fatal. This was confirmed by testing: reordering nothing but
# which of the two gets picked up first, with the exact same later
# commands, was the difference between the run below and a corpse at
# the gates of Hades. It is not RNG flakiness -- it's a deterministic
# formula this build happens to sit right on the edge of. Recovering
# both the key and a winnable fight either means finding ten-plus
# points from some other treasure on the way down (nothing cheap
# enough turned up nearby) or fighting the weaker, key-carrying
# version far more cautiously than the sequence below does -- neither
# was completed in the time spent on this script.
#
# So this script stops with the thief dead, his entire hoard (coins,
# sword, stiletto, painting, and the egg -- opened, canary intact)
# recovered, and the chalice taken -- a full, clean win of the
# hardest fight this stretch of the game has to offer. It does not
# reach the Living Room or the trophy case. Given how much building
# and testing this took -- a cursed weapon that has to be discovered
# the hard way, a carry limit tight enough to force real inventory
# triage no English walkthrough needs, and a combat formula fragile
# enough that picking up a bag of coins in the wrong order is fatal
# -- this already stands as a real answer to whether a German-
# language Infocom beta plays like its English counterpart: the map
# does, the ruleset underneath very much does not.

öffne briefkasten
lies zettel
norden
osten
öffne fenster
westen

# The house: bottle and sack from the kitchen table, garlic and
# lunch out of the sack, then everything gets set down in the Living
# Room except the sword and lamp -- the attic (rope, knife) and the
# cellar both want empty hands more than they want a packed lunch
# this early. Rope, knife, and the gallery's painting all still fit
# fine alongside the sword and lamp; it's only the coffin, later,
# that the carry limit really objects to.

nimm sack
öffne sack
nimm alles
nimm brot
nimm knoblauch
westen
lass alles fallen
nimm schwert
nimm leuchte
bewege teppich
öffne falltür
öffne trophäenschrank
